    "text": "Let's review and correct any potential errors in the solution step-by-step.\n\nWe start with the quadratic equation \\(x^2 - 3x + 1 = 0\\) and find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\\[\na, b = \\frac{3 \\pm \\sqrt{5}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{b}} + \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{a}}\\):\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{b}} + \\frac{b}{\\sqrt{a}} = \\frac{a\\sqrt{a} + b\\sqrt{b}}{\\sqrt{ab}}.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(ab = 1\\), this simplifies to:\n\\[\na\\sqrt{a} + b\\sqrt{b}.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(a^{3/2} + b^{3/2}\\). Using the identity for the sum of powers of roots of a quadratic equation, we know:\n\\[\na^{n+2} + b^{n+2} = (a + b)(a^{n+1} + b^{n+1}) - ab(a^n + b^n).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 = (a + b)(a^2 + b^2) - ab(a + b).\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(a + b = 3\\) and \\(ab = 1\\):\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 = (a + b)^2 - 2ab = 3^2 - 2 \\cdot 1 = 9 - 2 = 7.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 = 3 \\cdot 7 - 1 \\cdot 3 = 21 - 3 = 18.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 2\\):\n\\[\na^4 + b^4 = (a + b)(a^3 + b^3) - ab(a^2 + b^2).\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(a^3 + b^3 = 18\\) and \\(a^2 + b^2 = 7\\):\n\\[\na^4 + b^4 = 3 \\cdot 18 - 1 \\cdot 7 = 54 - 7 = 47.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 3\\):\n\\[\na^5 + b^5 = (a + b)(a^4 + b^4) - ab(a^3 + b^3).\n\\]\n\nWe already know \\(a^4 + b^4 = 47\\) and \\(a^3 + b^3 = 18\\):\n\\[\na^5 + b^5 = 3 \\cdot 47 - 1 \\cdot 18 = 141 - 18 = 123.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a^{3/2} + b^{3/2}\\). We use the recurrence relation:\n\\[\na^{n+2} + b^{n+2} = (a + b)(a^{n+1} + b^{n+1}) - ab(a^n + b^n).\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[\na^3 + b^3 = 18.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 2\\):\n\\[\na^4 + b^4 = 47.\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n = 1\\):\n\\[\na^{3/2} + b^{3/2} = \\sqrt{18} + \\sqrt{47}.\n\\]\n\nThus:\n\\[\na^{3/2} + b^{3/2} = \\sqrt{18} + \\sqrt{47}.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{18} + \\sqrt{47}}.\n\\]",
